<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Llywarch ap Llywelyn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 1173–1220</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Prydydd y Moch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Llywarch ap Llywelyn, known as Prydydd y Moch ('Poet of the Pigs'), was one of the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poets-of-the-princes">Poets of the Princes</a> in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, active from about 1173 to 1220. He composed elaborate praise poems for the rulers of Gwynedd, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn the Great</a>, whose rise he celebrated. His work forms a major part of the surviving poetry of the Gogynfeirdd.</p></div>
